# Independent review (read-only, once) of build 23 of lane tree_rebuild (MINIMAL launch mode), changed files only, versus build 21g

You review; you do not fix or design fixes. You run once, read-only, on trinity-1-18; nobody answers questions. Report only verified problems, each with file:line or artifact path and the command or reading that shows it. Your final message is the verdict.

## Context
Vehicle N's production proof (mode q on P's step-400 export) cannot use N's new routing slices yet (build 21h: `/scratch/jjyeung/orch_n30/tree_rebuild10_w/w21/out/BUILD21H_REPORT.md`). The orchestrator ruled MINIMAL: N trains on P's own routing stream (S1 base regenerated by `s1_routing.build`; P's step-400 routing receipt ca0616db..., database 4e8e6c04...) with N's removal lists and VQA changes; every new routing slice enters at N's first boundary. Build 23 (prompt `/scratch/jjyeung/orch_n30/tree_rebuild10_w/prompts/build23.md`, report `w23/out/BUILD23_REPORT.md`, package `w23/out/tree_rebuild_b23_*.tar`, patch `w23/out/MINIMAL_TREE.patch`) adds one sealed-tree change and a new kit q. Base package: `w21/out/tree_rebuild_b21g_2f9a23be.tar` (tree b262e7a7, kit q 7ecf6897).

## Check (orchestrator's conditions, RULING 1259Z item 2)
1. Compare the 21g and 23 packages member by member in memory (tarfile; write nothing); review only changed files. List every changed tree file; anything beyond the MINIMAL declaration (and, if the report says it was needed, the STUDENT_R2_FRAME_HOST env alias) is a finding.
2. The declaration is explicit, has one accepted value, and is pinned in the config identity (changing or removing it changes the identity the resolver and admission check). Without it every earlier refusal is unchanged (R1_PROOF_NATIVE_ROUTING_REQUIRED, R1_ROUTING_COMPOSITION_INPUTS_MISSING, compose_native's checks). With it: a kit that binds any slice cell or routing_inputs refuses by name; deferred cells hold zero rows in population, loader and card; routing_base must equal the regenerated S1 selection; removals, K1/room rules, held-out wording exclusions and the DEFERRED boundary still apply to every row.
3. Tests: failing-before/passing-after are real (run them against the 21g tree in memory or by reading); each new refusal has a mutation that fails without the check.
4. Kit q: name scan of the decoded kit clean; kit dir file list equals its SHA256SUMS; resolver identity checks unchanged; any new upload is pinned and small or already on Orchard (report names them).
5. Look-ahead and fixture: is the furthest stage claimed actually reached, with stand-ins only at GPU/weights/production stores/frame host? Does the MINIMAL capacity fixture ADMIT with every rank's finite first-batch loss?
6. Nothing changed in admission, legality, P's opener rows (routing_opener_init=keep), the step-544 lineage, the resolver's identity checks; GO stays false. Say what you did not check.

## Rules
Read-only. No ssh, network, GPU, Git, tmux, scheduler or another agent. Never list, search or grep on /home or /data*. Any process under nice -n 10, at most 8 threads, under 20 GB RAM. Verdict, first line: `GO`, `GO AFTER FIXES` (each fix MUST or SHOULD, with evidence) or `NO-GO`. Then at most 30 lines.
EOPR
